{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed218530468/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6d1d5c90\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nRemove the left control column and roll the remaining image downward by the red marker's row.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a rectangular grid with a one-cell-wide control column on the left.\n- That control column is all pink except for exactly one red marker pixel.\n- The columns to the right are the image that remains in the output.\n\nRule\n- Delete the left control column.\n- Let r be the row index of the red marker in that column.\n- Cyclically shift the remaining image downward by r rows.\n\nKey insight\n- The red marker's vertical position encodes the row-roll amount; the pink\n  column is only a control strip and is removed after reading it.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6350f1f4\" lines=\"62-81\">\n<description>\nDecode a block-grid image by swapping each sprite-coded cell with a solid cell and each blank cell with the sprite.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is partitioned by black separator rows and columns into equal-size\nsquare blocks. Each block is either a copy of one two-color sprite or a\nuniform blank block of the sprite's minority color; grey pixels are erased\ncells that act as wildcards.\n\nRule\nInfer the repeated sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks. Replace a\nblock matching that sprite, ignoring grey erasures, with a solid block of\nthe sprite's majority color. Replace a uniform blank block with the sprite.\nSeparator rows and columns, including grey erasures on them, become black.\n\nKey insight\nThe small sprite is a block-level code for one macro-cell state, and the\nuniform minority-color block is the other state; the output renders the\nopposite code in each macro-cell while cleaning erased pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\" lines=\"111-125\">\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\nKey insight: The visible floor has an invisible one-row cushion above it, so the objects fall under gravity but stop with one empty row before the floor or the grid bottom.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"981add89\" lines=\"10-29\">\n<description>\nExtend each top-row control pixel downward as a vertical toggle ray in its own color.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Non-background pixels on the top row are control pixels, each identifying\n  one column and one ray color.\n- Foreground regions below may intersect these control columns.\n\nRule:\n- For every top-row control pixel, process the cells below it in the same\n  column down to the bottom edge.\n- A cell that already has the control color is erased to the background.\n- Every other cell on that downward ray is painted with the control color.\n- The top-row control pixels themselves remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe top row is a set of vertical ray launchers; each ray toggles only cells\nmatching its own color and overwrites all other colors along that column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d93c6891\" lines=\"84-101\">\n<description>\nMove each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.\n- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.\n- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.\n\nRule\n- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.\n- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.\n- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.\n- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.\n- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"446ef5d2\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered rectangular pieces into a rectangle at an L-marker corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg rectangular pieces scattered on the grid.\n- 3-pixel L-marker (2x2 minus one cell) tags the corner; the gap is the corner cell.\n\nRule:\n- rect_col = most common color across all piece pixels; all outer rectangle edges must be rect_col.\n- Translate pieces (no rotation) to tile the rectangle; touching edges must match, with rect_col as wildcard.\n\nKey insight: rect_col acts as wildcard during edge matching — only non-rect_col pixels at shared boundaries must agree.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f28a3cbb\" lines=\"42-50\">\n<description>\nCompress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.\nInput structure\n- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.\n- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.\nRule\n- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.\nKey insight\n- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c61be7dc\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nFlatten the interior object into a centered line and move the guide bars inward to bracket it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- One non-background guide color touches the boundary and forms exactly two full parallel bars plus a split perpendicular center bar.\n- The other non-background color is a single 8-connected interior object whose area determines the flattened line length.\n\nRule:\n- If the guide pair is vertical, make a vertical object-colored line centered between those bars; otherwise use the analogous horizontal line.\n- The line length equals the number of interior object pixels and is centered on the split perpendicular guide bar.\n- Rebuild the guide color as the perpendicular center bar plus two full bars immediately beside the flattened object line.\n\nKey insight:\n- The guide bars supply only the orientation and center; the interior object's pixel count supplies the length of the final one-cell-thick line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"52364a65\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nErase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.\n\nRule:\n- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.\n- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db615bd4\" lines=\"111-130\">\n<description>\nThicken the sparse frame and stamp the external sparse sprites into it in positional order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a regular lattice color, one large\nsparse rectangular frame, and one or more external monochrome sprites whose\npixels also sit on a two-step lattice.\n\nRule\nUse the largest sparse rectangular perimeter as the frame. Clear that frame\nregion and the external sprite pixels to background, draw a solid one-pixel\nborder in the frame color, solidify each sparse sprite into its contiguous\ntwo-step mask, and place the sprites inside the frame with equal gaps.\nWide frames receive sprites left-to-right by source position; tall frames\nreceive them top-to-bottom by source position.\n\nKey insight\nThe frame supplies ordered slots: the sprite-to-slot assignment is by visible\nsource order, not by numeric color or by matching individual cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\" lines=\"97-117\">\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f14c03\" lines=\"75-83\">\n<description>\nCrop the foreground object whose missing cells match marker shapes, and fill those missing cells with the marker colors.\n\nInput structure\nA dominant background surrounds several monochromatic foreground objects. Exactly one object has background gaps inside its bounding box whose component shapes are matched by separate marker objects.\nRule\nFind that object, crop its bounding box, and replace each background gap component with the color of the separate marker component or components having the same normalized shape.\nKey insight\nThe marker objects do not mark absolute positions; their shapes identify which gaps in the selected object should be colored, while objects with unmatched gaps are distractors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"271d71e2\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove bordered containers toward same-colored destination markers, charging gray cells to orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 6 (magenta). Black-bordered (0) rectangles with gray (5) / orange (7) interiors.\n- A head sprite (9) sits adjacent to one container edge; a matching destination sprite lies along the movement axis.\n\nRule:\n- Each container translates toward its destination along the head-to-dest axis.\n- Steps = min(head-to-dest distance, number of gray interior cells).\n- Gray cells become orange from the head-facing side inward, one per step.\n\nKey insight: Gray cell count caps movement — a container stops short if it runs out of gray cells to charge.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c803e39c\" lines=\"31-40\">\n<description>\nExpand the second mask into a block grid by stamping the first mask with the two payload colors.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
